00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.560
So without further
ado, let's get

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.550
to answering your questions.

00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.730
The first question
is about fluoride.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:53.080
And the specific question is,
why is fluoride in our water,

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:55.570
and how much is it harming us?

00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:58.057
OK, well, that might seem
like a short question,

00:01:58.057 --> 00:01:59.890
but there's actually a
lot to that question.

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:03.940
And I want to be very clear,
by posing the question, how

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.370
much is it harming
us, it implies

00:02:06.370 --> 00:02:08.080
that fluoride is harming us.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:10.900
And I'd want to be very clear.

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:15.130
The degree to which fluoride
can harm you or not harm you

00:02:15.130 --> 00:02:17.230
depends on how
much of it happens

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:20.680
to be in the water or
toothpaste or some other thing

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:22.150
that you're consuming, OK?

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:23.360
So this is very important.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.440
I don't want the question to
seem like a leading question

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.110
to imply that fluoride is
necessarily harming us,

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:31.090
because as I'm
about to tell you,

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:35.230
there are clear benefits of
fluoride for tooth strength

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:37.300
and for warding off cavities.

00:02:37.300 --> 00:02:40.790
But if you get too much
fluoride into your system,

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:42.470
it is dangerous.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:44.842
It is a poison at
certain levels.

00:02:44.842 --> 00:02:46.300
So as you've probably
heard before,

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:48.710
the dose makes the poison.

00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:51.280
So let's take this
seemingly simple question

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:53.200
and dissect it in two.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.060
The first part of
the question was,

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.760
why is fluoride in our water?

00:02:57.760 --> 00:02:59.500
And believe it or
not, even that is

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.860
hard to answer directly
because here's what's

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.860
happening right now in 2024.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:09.490
There is a major lawsuit
in the state of California

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:14.010
to try and get fluoride removed
from the drinking water.

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:16.560
The outcome of that case is
still yet to be determined.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.150
But the case was filed
by a group of individuals

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:23.780
who strongly believe that
fluoride at any concentration

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:25.850
in the drinking water is bad.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:27.810
Why would they say that?

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:29.107
And what is their evidence?

00:03:29.107 --> 00:03:30.440
And how strong is that evidence?

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:34.580
Well, there is some evidence
that if levels of fluoride

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.160
in drinking water exceed
a certain threshold,

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.370
it can cause problems with
thyroid hormone function

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:43.560
and perhaps even certain
aspects of brain function.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.130
Again, if the levels
exceed a certain threshold.

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.730
So in the state of
California, there

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:51.230
is a major lawsuit
to try and get

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.520
fluoride removed from
the drinking water.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.520
Now, at the same time,
meaning right now,

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.980
there is also a major lawsuit
in the United States--

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.170
this one taking place
in Buffalo, New York--

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.410
whereby citizens
are suing the city

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:06.920
because they insist
that there is not

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.110
enough fluoride in the water.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:12.470
And they are suing for damages
based on the dental health

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:15.740
needs of their children that
did not, in their opinion,

00:04:15.740 --> 00:04:17.600
get enough fluoride
in the drinking water.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.100
So I'm telling you
about all of this

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:22.130
because the question, again, was
why is fluoride in our drinking

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:22.640
water?

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.980
And believe it or not, there
are certain states in the United

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:26.900
States, certain areas
of the United States,

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.890
where the fluoride levels in
the drinking water are low,

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.660
are zero, perhaps.

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:34.790
That's what the lawsuit
in Buffalo is about.

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:37.550
And by the way,
the city of Buffalo

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.850
may have now corrected
the amount of fluoride

00:04:40.850 --> 00:04:43.028
in the water, taking it
from zero to something.

00:04:43.028 --> 00:04:45.320
Whether or not it was actually
zero is still debatable.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.195
But I think you're
starting to get the point,

00:04:47.195 --> 00:04:50.300
that there is a wide,
wide divergence in terms

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.490
of how safe people
feel about having

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:54.300
fluoride in drinking water.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:56.518
Some people want it
and are suing cities

00:04:56.518 --> 00:04:58.310
because they feel there
wasn't enough of it

00:04:58.310 --> 00:04:59.750
in their drinking water.

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.660
And others don't want any
fluoride in their drinking

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:04.880
water, and they're suing
cities because of that.

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.080
So this is a really barbed-wire
topic, as I like to say.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:10.520
It's one that no
matter how close

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:13.110
you get to it, whatever
angle you look at it,

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:15.410
you're likely to get
stuck a little bit.

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:17.030
It's going to be a
little bit painful.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:22.010
OK, why is fluoride in
some drinking water at all?

00:05:22.010 --> 00:05:24.440
Let's set aside the levels
and just answer that question.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:27.590
Well, in an upcoming
episode of the Huberman Lab

00:05:27.590 --> 00:05:28.970
podcast about oral health.

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.140
I'll get into this
in a lot more detail.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.920
But fluoride is a really
interesting compound

00:05:33.920 --> 00:05:35.430
with respect to oral health.

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:36.560
And here's why.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.770
Your teeth, believe
it or not, are always

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:42.770
in a state of either
demineralization

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:45.082
or remineralization.

00:05:45.082 --> 00:05:47.540
Those are tricky words to say,
go ahead and try and say it.

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:49.700
Demineralization,
remineralization.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:53.570
So I'm going to refer to them as
"demin" for "demineralization"

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:56.930
and "remin" for
"remineralization.

00:05:56.930 --> 00:06:00.170
OK, your teeth are always
in one state or the other,

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:02.550
or one state predominates
versus the other.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:07.550
Now, a cavity in your mouth
occurs when bacteria--

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:10.880
in particular,
streptococcus mutans,

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.400
sometimes called strep mutans--

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:17.540
feeds on sugars in
your mouth and then,

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.150
as a byproduct of
that feeding, creates

00:06:20.150 --> 00:06:24.020
acids that then erode the
enamel and deeper layers

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:25.020
of your teeth.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:28.280
In other words, it
demineralizes the tooth.

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:29.780
Now, I just told
you that your teeth

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:34.570
can be in a state of demin
or remin at any moment.

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.550
The way that your teeth remin,
the way they remineralize

00:06:38.550 --> 00:06:41.790
and can potentially fill in
little cavities that have not

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.190
yet made it to the deeper
layers of your teeth,

00:06:44.190 --> 00:06:47.310
is through a process that
involves the construction

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:49.590
of crystals from minerals--

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.200
that's why it's called
remineralization--

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:53.640
and get this.

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:54.570
This really wild.

00:06:54.570 --> 00:06:57.270
These minerals
actually stack together

00:06:57.270 --> 00:06:59.340
in very organized
little sequences,

00:06:59.340 --> 00:07:00.720
kind of like LEGO blocks.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:04.710
And the critical element
within those blocks

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:06.837
is something called
hydroxyapatite.

00:07:06.837 --> 00:07:08.670
I love that it has the
word "appetite" in it

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:10.337
because we're talking
about oral health.

00:07:10.337 --> 00:07:11.940
That's the normal process.

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:15.300
Fluoride was discovered,
not because it's a vitamin,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.040
not because it's an
essential nutrient.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:19.530
In fact, at high concentrations,
it's actually a poison.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.690
I'll tell you a story about
that in a moment, where

00:07:21.690 --> 00:07:24.000
I was actually poisoned
by fluoride at the dentist

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.810
when I was a kid, in large
part due to my own error.

00:07:28.810 --> 00:07:31.840
Fluoride, it was
discovered, can actually

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:35.710
get inside of those LEGO
chain-like crystals,

00:07:35.710 --> 00:07:39.100
the building of those, and
form bonds between them

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:42.970
that are actually stronger than
the hydroxyapatite bonds that

00:07:42.970 --> 00:07:44.270
would normally form.

00:07:44.270 --> 00:07:50.500
In other words, if you consume
fluoridated water or use

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:52.870
toothpaste with
fluoride, especially

00:07:52.870 --> 00:07:55.690
if you do this when you're a
kid, what ends up happening

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:00.040
is that the crystals and
the mineralization structure

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:03.370
of the teeth becomes
ultrastrong, meaning

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.880
supraphysiologically
strong, which then limits

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:09.040
the ability for
that strep mutans

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.370
and the acid strep mutans
produces when it eats sugar

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.980
to erode the enamel
of your teeth

00:08:14.980 --> 00:08:17.620
causing what we
refer to as cavities.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:21.700
OK, so I went and asked several
dentists and a periodontist

00:08:21.700 --> 00:08:23.870
about, why is fluoride
in drinking water?

00:08:23.870 --> 00:08:25.630
Why is fluoride in toothpaste?

00:08:25.630 --> 00:08:27.580
And they, of course,
gave me the explanation

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:32.179
that I just gave you, which is a
chemical mechanical explanation

00:08:32.179 --> 00:08:33.320
or rationale.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.510
However, most of the
things for our health,

00:08:36.510 --> 00:08:39.350
such as the foods we eat, the
amount of exercise that we do,

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:43.250
are not naturally put into
our environment, right?

00:08:43.250 --> 00:08:47.180
But tap water, which goes out
to everyone who consumes it

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:52.800
from the tap, is basically
a government or local

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:54.600
government-supplied resource.

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.640
And basically, the
rationale was--

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:58.980
and I believe this took
place first in the 1950s--

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:01.380
was, OK, given the potential
for fluoride to make

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:04.860
supraphysiologically
strong teeth, what can we

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:07.560
do to reduce the
cavities and tooth

00:09:07.560 --> 00:09:11.010
decay that would occur in
children and adult populations?

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.540
Let's put fluoride in
the drinking water.

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:14.550
So that's what they did.

00:09:14.550 --> 00:09:16.740
They did not do
this, I was told,

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:20.040
because it was necessarily the
best way to take care of teeth

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.150
and avoid cavities.

00:09:21.150 --> 00:09:23.067
It turns out there are
a bunch of other things

00:09:23.067 --> 00:09:26.760
that you can do, even if you
consume no fluoridated water

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.020
or toothpaste.

00:09:28.020 --> 00:09:30.990
There are things that you can
do to enhance the mineralization

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.510
state of your teeth, even
fill in cavities, provided

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:35.850
those cavities haven't
made it into the deeper

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.650
so-called dentine
layers of the teeth.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:39.970
I'll get into all of this
in that future episode.

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:42.632
But what the US and other
governments decided--

00:09:42.632 --> 00:09:44.340
this, of course, varies
across the world,

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:46.882
to the extent to which there's
fluoride in the drinking water

00:09:46.882 --> 00:09:49.900
or not-- was that by putting
fluoride in the drinking water,

00:09:49.900 --> 00:09:52.240
they could prevent a
large amount of tooth

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:55.600
decay and cavities that
would otherwise occur.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:59.770
It was, and remains, a
fairly low-cost approach

00:09:59.770 --> 00:10:02.450
for these cities to introduce
fluoride to the drinking water.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:04.900
And that's why fluoride
is in drinking water.

00:10:04.900 --> 00:10:08.300
It is to try and create
supraphysiologically strong

00:10:08.300 --> 00:10:08.800
teeth.

00:10:08.800 --> 00:10:10.630
And, indeed, every
single dentist I

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:13.150
spoke to, including
functional dentists.

00:10:13.150 --> 00:10:14.230
I spoke to periodontists.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:16.000
I spoke to several
dentists, ranging

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.800
from, let's call them, more
"traditionally trained"

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:20.217
dentists to more, let's call
them, "alternative dentists."

00:10:20.217 --> 00:10:23.710
All of them agree that at a
chemical mechanical level,

00:10:23.710 --> 00:10:26.260
fluoride creates stronger teeth.

00:10:26.260 --> 00:10:28.120
However, every
single one of them

00:10:28.120 --> 00:10:30.460
also acknowledged
that the bonds that

00:10:30.460 --> 00:10:33.717
are created in those
mineralization chains, as I'm

00:10:33.717 --> 00:10:34.300
referring to--

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.550
I realize that's not
the technical term--

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:42.190
is not the normal hydroxyapatite
bonds that would form.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:44.800
They are stronger than the
bonds that would normally form.

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:46.187
They are structurally different.

00:10:46.187 --> 00:10:48.770
If you look at them down what's
called an electron microscope,

00:10:48.770 --> 00:10:50.728
you'll see that they're
structurally different.

00:10:50.728 --> 00:10:53.350
And some, not all of the
dentists, I spoke to said,

00:10:53.350 --> 00:10:55.810
yeah, it would be
best to remineralize

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:57.550
the teeth to fill
in any cavities

00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:58.810
that initially have formed--

00:10:58.810 --> 00:11:00.130
again, not down to
the deep layers,

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:01.930
but they could still
be filled in naturally

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:06.340
through the building up of those
hydroxyapatite natural bonds.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:09.970
But they acknowledged that many
people, perhaps most people,

00:11:09.970 --> 00:11:13.360
don't take adequate care of
their mouths and their teeth.

00:11:13.360 --> 00:11:16.510
So they understood the
rationale of putting fluoride

00:11:16.510 --> 00:11:17.560
into drinking water.

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:19.450
And of course, that's
also why fluoride is

00:11:19.450 --> 00:11:21.580
in many, not all toothpastes.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.130
OK, so I'm hoping that
clearly answers for you

00:11:24.130 --> 00:11:27.010
the question of why there is
fluoride in our drinking water

00:11:27.010 --> 00:11:27.670
at all.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:29.890
At least in most
American cities,

00:11:29.890 --> 00:11:31.570
there is fluoride in
the drinking water,

00:11:31.570 --> 00:11:34.570
although this
lawsuit in Buffalo,

00:11:34.570 --> 00:11:38.470
I guess soon to be determined,
will probably tell us

00:11:38.470 --> 00:11:40.883
whether or not, indeed,
there was a total lack

00:11:40.883 --> 00:11:43.300
or a partial lack of fluoride
in the drinking water there.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:45.360
I'm presuming that the
accusation is true.

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:47.110
But I don't actually
know that to be true.

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:48.790
I just know that there's
a lawsuit that exists.

00:11:48.790 --> 00:11:51.332
If you Google that, you can see
some YouTube videos about it.

00:11:51.332 --> 00:11:54.050
There's information
about this happening.

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:55.610
There are a bunch
of angry parents.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:57.902
And I'm sure there are angry
parents on the other side.

00:11:57.902 --> 00:12:00.460
And I don't know what the
kids are saying, but hopefully

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:03.533
no matter what, they're saying
them through healthy teeth.

00:12:03.533 --> 00:12:05.200
OK, now, the second
half of the question

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:09.063
was, how much is the fluoride
in drinking water harming us?

00:12:09.063 --> 00:12:11.230
I'm going to answer this
question very specifically.

00:12:11.230 --> 00:12:14.050
The person asks, how much is
the fluoride in our drinking

00:12:14.050 --> 00:12:15.340
water harming us?

00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:17.530
Well, I already told you
ways in which it is helping

00:12:17.530 --> 00:12:19.750
the strength of your teeth.

00:12:19.750 --> 00:12:21.592
That's not debated.

00:12:21.592 --> 00:12:23.050
Again, there might
be some dentists

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:26.320
that say, oh, the bonds
that are formed by fluoride

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.330
are different than the
ones that are naturally

00:12:28.330 --> 00:12:30.580
created with no fluoride,
and so there might

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:31.670
be some debate about that.

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:33.910
But all of them acknowledge
that those bonds

00:12:33.910 --> 00:12:36.310
and the mineralization
of the teeth

00:12:36.310 --> 00:12:38.020
is stronger with fluoride.

00:12:38.020 --> 00:12:40.450
They understand and
agree with the rationale,

00:12:40.450 --> 00:12:43.000
even if they don't necessarily
agree with the practice

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.100
as the best practice.

00:12:45.100 --> 00:12:48.550
How much is fluoride in
drinking water harming you?

00:12:48.550 --> 00:12:51.010
Well, there are two
things you need to ask.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:53.260
One is, how much fluoride
are you actually drinking?

00:12:53.260 --> 00:12:55.600
Because it turns out
there's a tremendous range

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.790
of fluoride concentrations
in tap water,

00:12:57.790 --> 00:12:59.530
depending on what
city you live in.

00:12:59.530 --> 00:13:02.470
Now, I did an entire episode
of the Huberman Lab podcast

00:13:02.470 --> 00:13:03.830
about water.

00:13:03.830 --> 00:13:06.212
We talked about distilled
water versus spring water.

00:13:06.212 --> 00:13:07.670
We talked about
hydrogenated water.

00:13:07.670 --> 00:13:08.840
We talked about alkaline water.

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:10.340
We talked about all
of those things.

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:11.650
And we did talk about fluoride.

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:14.090
And one of the
key takeaways when

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:16.090
you're thinking about
fluoride in drinking water

00:13:16.090 --> 00:13:19.540
is to know that if fluoride
concentrations in drinking

00:13:19.540 --> 00:13:22.180
water are too high or--

00:13:22.180 --> 00:13:24.100
and this is a very
important "or"--

00:13:24.100 --> 00:13:27.820
you're consuming a lot of
a particular water that

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:30.137
contains even low levels
of fluoride, well, there's

00:13:30.137 --> 00:13:32.470
the potential-- and, again,
I want to highlight the word

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:33.220
"potential" here.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:35.140
I don't want to cause alarm.

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:40.210
That the fluoride is causing
disruptions to thyroid hormone

00:13:40.210 --> 00:13:44.230
output or on the receptor end,
in other words disruptions

00:13:44.230 --> 00:13:47.892
to thyroid hormone metabolism
and usage in the body.

00:13:47.892 --> 00:13:49.350
OK, I want to be
really clear here.

00:13:49.350 --> 00:13:51.270
I don't want people
to think, oh, fluoride

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:52.860
destroys your thyroid hormone.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:55.800
It's the dose that
makes the poison.

00:13:55.800 --> 00:13:57.090
It depends on how much.

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:01.620
Now, the Center for Disease
Control have set a recommended

00:14:01.620 --> 00:14:09.960
level of 0.7mg/l of fluoride in
drinking water as the level--

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:13.620
and here I'm paraphrasing-- that
can help prevent tooth decay

00:14:13.620 --> 00:14:17.260
and promote good oral health
and that they have deemed safe.

00:14:17.260 --> 00:14:20.700
But if you want to know how much
fluoride is in your drinking

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:24.570
water, whether or not it is
above, at, or below that value,

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.880
you need to get your
tap water tested.

00:14:26.880 --> 00:14:29.520
Now, fortunately,
there are websites

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.470
that can tell you
how much fluoride

00:14:31.470 --> 00:14:32.670
is in your drinking water.

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.860
And if you trust the
data on those websites,

00:14:34.860 --> 00:14:36.360
you can simply put
the city you live

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.588
in into one of those
websites, and you'll

00:14:38.588 --> 00:14:39.630
get the information back.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:41.820
I'll provide a few links
to those different websites

00:14:41.820 --> 00:14:43.810
in the Show Note captions
for this episode.

00:14:43.810 --> 00:14:46.350
We also provided those links
in the Show Note captions

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:48.240
for the episode on Water.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:49.180
And then you can see.

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:50.805
Are you adjusting
the level of fluoride

00:14:50.805 --> 00:14:52.720
in your drinking water
that's deemed safe,

00:14:52.720 --> 00:14:55.023
less than that value,
or more than that value?

00:14:55.023 --> 00:14:56.440
But keep in mind
that if you drink

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:58.420
a little bit, a moderate
amount, or a lot

00:14:58.420 --> 00:15:00.760
of fluoridated
drinking water, you're

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:03.048
going to be consuming either
more or less fluoride.

00:15:03.048 --> 00:15:04.840
Just because there's
a concentration that's

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:07.810
been deemed safe by the
Center for Disease Control

00:15:07.810 --> 00:15:10.588
does not actually tell
you whether or not,

00:15:10.588 --> 00:15:12.880
OK, if you're drinking half
a gallon of tap water a day

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:15.190
versus a gallon versus
just a couple of cups,

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.613
whether or not you
are in that safe zone.

00:15:17.613 --> 00:15:19.780
And of course, this is going
to vary by body weight.

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:23.920
If you're a small child,
those lower levels of fluoride

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:28.930
are going to equate to a
larger total amount of fluoride

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:31.420
as seen by the
volume of the body.

00:15:31.420 --> 00:15:33.153
If you think about
this, everything's

00:15:33.153 --> 00:15:34.070
by body weight volume.

00:15:34.070 --> 00:15:37.000
And this is why drug dosages
are most typically calculated

00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:40.330
as a function of pounds or
kilograms of body weight.

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:43.750
Not everybody gets the
same dose of every drug.

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:45.340
And if you're a
very large person,

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:47.320
maybe you can tolerate
more fluoride.

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:49.810
As I mentioned before,
high levels of fluoride

00:15:49.810 --> 00:15:52.340
have been shown to disrupt
thyroid metabolism.

00:15:52.340 --> 00:15:54.910
It has also been
hypothesized-- again,

00:15:54.910 --> 00:15:57.310
this is still highly
debated-- hypothesized

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:59.740
to be neurotoxic under
certain conditions.

00:15:59.740 --> 00:16:01.510
That is, toxic to neurons.

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:03.940
It can kill neurons at
certain concentrations,

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:05.380
not necessarily
the concentrations

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:07.270
present in your drinking water.

00:16:07.270 --> 00:16:10.300
A lot of the evidence that
fluoride is neurotoxic

00:16:10.300 --> 00:16:12.440
is from so-called
in vitro studies.

00:16:12.440 --> 00:16:14.050
So studies done
effectively in a dish,

00:16:14.050 --> 00:16:15.880
although there is
some in vivo evidence

00:16:15.880 --> 00:16:17.890
that it can cause a
neurotoxicity, a.k.a.

00:16:17.890 --> 00:16:19.550
neurodegeneration.

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:22.420
So I think when it comes
to the topic of fluoride,

00:16:22.420 --> 00:16:27.040
people tend to bin out into
not concerned and simply want

00:16:27.040 --> 00:16:29.620
fluoride to strengthen
their teeth; mildly concern,

00:16:29.620 --> 00:16:31.870
kind of keeping an eye on
this stuff-- you know, OK,

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:33.370
fluoride doesn't
sound great for me,

00:16:33.370 --> 00:16:35.562
but as long as the
concentrations aren't too high,

00:16:35.562 --> 00:16:38.020
some people will say, I've been
drinking tap water my whole

00:16:38.020 --> 00:16:41.030
life and I feel great, my teeth
are strong, and my brain works,

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:43.000
and my thyroid seems fine--

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:45.130
and then people who are
very, very concerned

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:48.020
about fluoride at any
concentration in their drinking

00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:48.520
water.

00:16:48.520 --> 00:16:51.340
Hence, the lawsuit in
California and other lawsuits

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:52.790
around the country.

00:16:52.790 --> 00:16:55.578
So I believe that when we're
talking about fluoride,

00:16:55.578 --> 00:16:57.370
you really need to
think about the dosages.

00:16:57.370 --> 00:16:59.740
And you need to ask yourself
which one of those three

00:16:59.740 --> 00:17:01.490
categories you fall into.

00:17:01.490 --> 00:17:05.200
Now, if you are
concerned about fluoride,

00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:08.150
it does not necessarily mean
that you can't drink tap water.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:11.740
The suggestion simply would
be to filter that tap water.

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:14.200
And during the episode
I did on water,

00:17:14.200 --> 00:17:16.780
I talked about a number
of different filtration

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:17.920
approaches.

00:17:17.920 --> 00:17:20.170
Many of those filters
will filter out fluoride,

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:22.630
and you can simply look
up water filters that

00:17:22.630 --> 00:17:24.250
eliminate or reduce fluoride.

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:27.099
And then some people have
enough disposable income

00:17:27.099 --> 00:17:29.620
and/or are concerned
enough about fluoride

00:17:29.620 --> 00:17:33.370
in their drinking water that
they will purchase or create

00:17:33.370 --> 00:17:36.610
very extensive, very
thorough filtration systems

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:39.500
to completely eliminate fluoride
from their drinking water.

00:17:39.500 --> 00:17:42.430
So you'll find different
ranges of concern.

00:17:42.430 --> 00:17:46.570
Again, aside from the data
of high fluoride levels

00:17:46.570 --> 00:17:48.760
being disruptive to
thyroid hormone pathways

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:52.570
and possibly
neurotoxic, I personally

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:56.980
am somebody who filters the
drinking water I consume out

00:17:56.980 --> 00:18:00.310
of the tap, unless that
is I'm going to boil water

00:18:00.310 --> 00:18:03.010
with it for making things like
loose leaf yerba mate which

00:18:03.010 --> 00:18:05.030
is one of my favorite
drinks, although lately I

00:18:05.030 --> 00:18:08.400
drink this cold brew Mateina
yerba mate, which by the way,

00:18:08.400 --> 00:18:11.540
is made with purified
water, no fluoride.

00:18:11.540 --> 00:18:13.020
And so on and so forth.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:15.080
But if I'm making rice
or I'm making pasta

00:18:15.080 --> 00:18:17.840
or I'm making oatmeal and
I need to use tap water,

00:18:17.840 --> 00:18:20.340
I don't worry about removing
the fluoride from that water.

00:18:20.340 --> 00:18:21.890
However, if I'm
going to drink water,

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:24.380
if I'm going to mix an element
electrolyte pack in or just

00:18:24.380 --> 00:18:26.810
drink a glass of water
or go out on a hike

00:18:26.810 --> 00:18:28.610
and take some
water with me, I do

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:32.030
use a water filter, either a
filter that fills from the top

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:34.730
and then seeps down, the ones
you put in the refrigerator.

00:18:34.730 --> 00:18:37.160
Or I recently purchased
a whole house filter

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:39.440
for the drinking
water taps in my house

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:41.277
so that it does remove
all the fluoride

00:18:41.277 --> 00:18:43.110
and removes some other
contaminants as well.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:45.020
So it's going to depend
on your level of concern,

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:47.180
and it's going to depend
on your disposable income

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:48.480
and any number of other things.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.230
And I must say that
every once in a while,

00:18:50.230 --> 00:18:52.430
I'll drink a little bit of
tap water out of the tap

00:18:52.430 --> 00:18:54.350
without any concern about
filtering the fluoride.

00:18:54.350 --> 00:18:56.892
I'm not somebody who gets hyper
concerned about these things.

00:18:56.892 --> 00:18:58.400
But I do understand
why some people

00:18:58.400 --> 00:19:00.770
do get hyper concerned about
these things, especially

00:19:00.770 --> 00:19:03.440
people who've read up on
fluoride and some of the health

00:19:03.440 --> 00:19:05.210
concerns of consuming
too much fluoride,

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:08.510
because I also find it very
logical and understandable

00:19:08.510 --> 00:19:10.168
that as people
learn more about how

00:19:10.168 --> 00:19:12.710
a particular substance might be
harming their brain or bodily

00:19:12.710 --> 00:19:14.538
health that they will
become more concerned

00:19:14.538 --> 00:19:15.830
about consuming that substance.

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:17.300
It just stands to reason.

00:19:17.300 --> 00:19:18.530
So what am I suggesting?

00:19:18.530 --> 00:19:21.230
I suggest that you figure
out how much fluoride

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.210
is in your tap water.

00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:25.220
I suggest that you,
then, make a decision

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:27.380
as to whether or not
to filter that water

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:29.848
or not before drinking it.

00:19:29.848 --> 00:19:31.390
And then, of course,
you have to make

00:19:31.390 --> 00:19:33.910
a decision about what sort
of financial investment

00:19:33.910 --> 00:19:35.950
you're willing to make
to filter that water.

00:19:35.950 --> 00:19:40.090
You can find a list of different
price ranges of water filters

00:19:40.090 --> 00:19:43.295
in the Show Note captions
in the Water episode.

00:19:43.295 --> 00:19:44.920
You may also want to
watch that episode

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:46.300
and go to that timestamp.

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.360
And there's an
enormous range there.

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:49.360
I want to be very clear.

00:19:49.360 --> 00:19:50.902
I don't have a
financial relationship

00:19:50.902 --> 00:19:53.290
to any of those
filtration mechanisms.

00:19:53.290 --> 00:19:55.990
Again, some people
have $0 to devote

00:19:55.990 --> 00:19:58.127
to that process of
taking the fluoride out

00:19:58.127 --> 00:19:59.710
of their water and
other contaminants.

00:19:59.710 --> 00:20:02.090
Some people have many, many
tens of thousands of dollars.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:05.050
So it really just depends
on your disposable income

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:06.230
and your level of concern.

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:08.230
But if you were to ask
me, I would say, yeah,

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:11.380
I think given that the cost
of most of the filters that

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:13.367
can remove most of
the fluoride is low,

00:20:13.367 --> 00:20:15.700
and given that there is some
health concern of consuming

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.760
too much fluoride, why
not just remove fluoride

00:20:18.760 --> 00:20:19.760
from the drinking water?

00:20:19.760 --> 00:20:22.210
And then if you say, well,
won't that weaken my teeth,

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:23.980
then I would say, well,
watch the episode that's

00:20:23.980 --> 00:20:26.290
soon to come out on oral health
because it's going to explain

00:20:26.290 --> 00:20:28.990
a lot of approaches, including
fluoride-containing toothpaste

00:20:28.990 --> 00:20:31.100
but some other
nonfluoride-containing

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:32.750
toothpaste and other
things that one

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:38.240
can do that the community of
professionally-trained dentists

00:20:38.240 --> 00:20:41.660
all agree can really help
improve the mineralization

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:44.390
state of your teeth and, indeed,
can fill in cavities that have

00:20:44.390 --> 00:20:45.932
begun to form but
haven't yet made it

00:20:45.932 --> 00:20:48.810
to the deeper layers of
your teeth, and on and on.

00:20:48.810 --> 00:20:50.780
Now, I do have a brief
story about fluoride

00:20:50.780 --> 00:20:53.420
that I'd be remiss if I didn't
tell you, which essentially

00:20:53.420 --> 00:20:55.640
shows that fluoride is
designed to strengthen teeth

00:20:55.640 --> 00:20:58.250
but is also indeed a poison.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:01.250
So when I was a kid, I had
a lot of dental issues.

00:21:01.250 --> 00:21:02.870
I didn't consume much sugar.

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:05.990
My mother, fortunately,
was good about not letting

00:21:05.990 --> 00:21:07.320
us consume too much sugar.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:10.200
But for whatever reason, my
mouth was kind of a mess.

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:10.700
I'd brush.

00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:11.210
I'd floss.

00:21:11.210 --> 00:21:13.100
I would do all these
things, but I actually

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:16.610
had my adult teeth come
in behind my baby teeth,

00:21:16.610 --> 00:21:18.620
so it didn't push
out my baby teeth.

00:21:18.620 --> 00:21:21.320
So not only did I not
collect from the tooth

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:24.000
fairy, which I only recently
discovered isn't real.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:25.215
But how would I know?

00:21:25.215 --> 00:21:26.840
Because the tooth
fairy never showed up

00:21:26.840 --> 00:21:29.840
because all of my adult teeth
came in behind my baby teeth.

00:21:29.840 --> 00:21:32.888
So I had to have all of my
baby teeth pulled on the top.

00:21:32.888 --> 00:21:34.430
So I did not have
a good relationship

00:21:34.430 --> 00:21:36.930
to the dentist as a
location or a person.

00:21:36.930 --> 00:21:39.980
In fact, they had to tell
me not to bite the dentist.

00:21:39.980 --> 00:21:42.568
By the way, I haven't bitten
a dentist in a long time.

00:21:42.568 --> 00:21:44.360
So hopefully, my dentist,
if watching this,

00:21:44.360 --> 00:21:45.840
is not too concerned.

00:21:45.840 --> 00:21:47.810
I do get twice a year cleanings.

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:50.670
I think every dentist agrees
that's a good thing to do.

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:52.350
Some people may need less.

00:21:52.350 --> 00:21:53.960
Some people may need more.

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:54.813
But how do you know?

00:21:54.813 --> 00:21:56.480
You need to go to a
dentist to find out.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:58.430
Back to the story
about fluoride.

00:21:58.430 --> 00:22:00.020
I started going to
the dentist quite

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:02.270
a bit because of all
these dental issues

00:22:02.270 --> 00:22:03.650
that I had as a kid.

00:22:03.650 --> 00:22:04.760
And what they used to do--

00:22:04.760 --> 00:22:05.720
I don't know if they
do this anymore--

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:06.830
but what they used to
do is they give you

00:22:06.830 --> 00:22:09.170
these little trays, which
are kind of like mouth guards

00:22:09.170 --> 00:22:10.880
that you would use
for boxing or hockey

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:12.390
so your teeth don't
get knocked out.

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:15.200
And they would fill those trays
with this jelly-like stuff that

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:17.480
contained fluoride, and
they put it on the top

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:18.350
and on the bottom.

00:22:18.350 --> 00:22:19.580
And the stuff just--

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:21.943
oh, little bits of it
would seep onto your tongue

00:22:21.943 --> 00:22:22.610
and your throat.

00:22:22.610 --> 00:22:23.660
And it was so sour.

00:22:23.660 --> 00:22:24.740
It was so awful.

00:22:24.740 --> 00:22:27.530
And they'd seat me
in this little wicker

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:30.215
chair in front of a television
and turn on cartoons,

00:22:30.215 --> 00:22:32.090
as if that was supposed
to make me forget how

00:22:32.090 --> 00:22:34.400
awful the whole experience was.

00:22:34.400 --> 00:22:37.070
If anything, it probably just
created a Pavlovian conditioned

00:22:37.070 --> 00:22:42.260
response to hate cartoons,
which I suppose did not work,

00:22:42.260 --> 00:22:43.550
because I liked cartoons then.

00:22:43.550 --> 00:22:45.570
I don't watch them now.

00:22:45.570 --> 00:22:47.142
Truly, I don't watch them now.

00:22:47.142 --> 00:22:48.600
So I sit down on
this wicker chair.

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:51.210
I've got the fluoride in my
teeth with these two trays.

00:22:51.210 --> 00:22:53.010
And I'm just hating
this whole process.

00:22:53.010 --> 00:22:55.218
I think I must have been
about five or six years old.

00:22:55.218 --> 00:22:57.900
So I decide, in my
infinite wisdom,

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:02.250
to just swallow all
the fluoride paste.

00:23:02.250 --> 00:23:05.300
So I start sucking it down
through these mouth guards.

00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:07.980
I can feel it going back into
my throat, and it's sour,

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:08.730
and it's stinging.

00:23:08.730 --> 00:23:11.240
It's awful, but I'm thinking,
OK, I'm just going to do this.

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:12.365
And I'm going to sit there.

00:23:12.365 --> 00:23:14.270
I'm basically going
to beat the test.

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:17.450
And so I drink all
this fluoride paste.

00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:20.090
And then, of course,
I got immensely sick

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:21.290
within about three minutes.

00:23:21.290 --> 00:23:21.793
I stood up.

00:23:21.793 --> 00:23:23.960
I turned around and puked
all over the wicker chair.

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:25.160
I think that was deliberate.

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:27.890
Actually, looking back,
it was deliberate.

00:23:27.890 --> 00:23:31.220
And puke all over
the wicker chair.

00:23:31.220 --> 00:23:32.840
My mom comes running in.

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:33.540
What happened?

00:23:33.540 --> 00:23:34.560
What did you do to him?

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:35.810
She was very protective of me.

00:23:35.810 --> 00:23:37.220
Thanks, Mom.

00:23:37.220 --> 00:23:38.270
What did you do to him?

00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:40.480
And then I said I just
swallowed the stuff.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:42.980
They wanted to do it again, and
I stopped doing the fluoride

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:44.010
treatment at that point.

00:23:44.010 --> 00:23:45.290
Why did I vomit?

00:23:45.290 --> 00:23:47.780
Well, I vomited because
fluoride is, indeed,

00:23:47.780 --> 00:23:49.850
a poison at high concentrations.

00:23:49.850 --> 00:23:51.530
Now, do I tell you
that story to make

00:23:51.530 --> 00:23:54.345
you afraid of giving your
kids fluoridated toothpaste

00:23:54.345 --> 00:23:55.220
or fluoridated water?

00:23:55.220 --> 00:23:58.370
No, by all means, do
what you think is best

00:23:58.370 --> 00:24:00.070
for you and for your children.

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:02.220
For me, however,
I've made some effort

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:04.920
to avoid fluoride toothpaste.

00:24:04.920 --> 00:24:08.232
I do go to the dentist, as I
mentioned, about once a year.

00:24:08.232 --> 00:24:10.440
I confess, during the
pandemic, it was probably less,

00:24:10.440 --> 00:24:14.590
as they were busy, and it was
hard to schedule, et cetera.

00:24:14.590 --> 00:24:19.180
I've had very, very few cavities
in my adult life, hardly any.

00:24:19.180 --> 00:24:21.220
And my gum health
is very strong,

00:24:21.220 --> 00:24:25.860
et cetera, et cetera, largely
through taking on protocols

00:24:25.860 --> 00:24:28.080
that I'm going to describe
in the Oral Health episode

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:29.910
and that were recommended
to me by friends

00:24:29.910 --> 00:24:32.612
who are both dentists and other
friends who are periodontists.

00:24:32.612 --> 00:24:34.320
Because, again, there
are a lot of things

00:24:34.320 --> 00:24:37.230
that we can do to strengthen
our teeth in natural ways,

00:24:37.230 --> 00:24:40.060
by building up those
hydroxyapatite bonds,

00:24:40.060 --> 00:24:42.360
which are the natural
bonds that teeth form.

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:43.890
And yes, believe
it or not, being

00:24:43.890 --> 00:24:45.785
able to reverse
some early-formed

00:24:45.785 --> 00:24:47.160
cavities, as long
as they haven't

00:24:47.160 --> 00:24:48.420
made it deep into the tooth.

00:24:48.420 --> 00:24:51.060
Also things like using
a soft toothbrush,

00:24:51.060 --> 00:24:53.920
because if you brush too hard,
if you take that approach

00:24:53.920 --> 00:24:55.140
and you're going to brush
your teeth really hard,

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:56.890
get them really, really
clean, yes, you'll

00:24:56.890 --> 00:24:59.460
scrape off all the biofilm,
you'll avoid tartar build up,

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:01.650
but you can really cause
some tenting of the gum

00:25:01.650 --> 00:25:04.680
tissue above the teeth and those
little recesses back in there

00:25:04.680 --> 00:25:05.740
where bacteria get in.

00:25:05.740 --> 00:25:09.000
And there's now a lot of
really strong evidence showing

00:25:09.000 --> 00:25:12.930
that some of that bacteria can
translate into cardiac disease,

00:25:12.930 --> 00:25:14.670
can translate into
metabolic disease,

00:25:14.670 --> 00:25:16.990
and maybe even into
some neurologic disease.

00:25:16.990 --> 00:25:17.910
So it's serious stuff.

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:21.300
Oral health is one of the most
important areas of health.

00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:23.220
And it's one of the
most overlooked areas

00:25:23.220 --> 00:25:23.970
of oral health.

00:25:23.970 --> 00:25:26.645
And I think people generally
fall into two categories.

00:25:26.645 --> 00:25:28.020
And I'll use these
two categories

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:30.450
to frame up the episode on
oral health that's coming.

00:25:30.450 --> 00:25:32.872
One category of
person seems really

00:25:32.872 --> 00:25:34.080
bullish on their oral health.

00:25:34.080 --> 00:25:35.640
They're like, keep my
teeth white and clean,

00:25:35.640 --> 00:25:37.890
and they floss and brush
twice a day, every day.

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:40.860
And they use tooth whiteners and
mouthwash and all those things.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:43.350
And the other category is
kind of blasé about it.

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:45.040
Yeah, I brush my
teeth in the morning

00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.310
so that my breath
doesn't kill every person

00:25:47.310 --> 00:25:50.800
I get into an interaction
with, and so on and so forth.

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:53.010
But in reality, both
the group of people

00:25:53.010 --> 00:25:55.020
that are doing an
immense number of things

00:25:55.020 --> 00:25:58.200
to try and keep their teeth
white and their breath fresh

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:01.330
are, yes, doing things that
are good for their oral health,

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:03.940
but also, no doubt,
damaging their oral health,

00:26:03.940 --> 00:26:06.210
in particular the
oral microbiome, which

00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:07.267
is absolutely critical.

00:26:07.267 --> 00:26:08.850
And then of course,
the other category

00:26:08.850 --> 00:26:12.600
of people that are
neglecting their oral hygiene

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.730
and are not taking mouthwashes
and things like that

00:26:14.730 --> 00:26:17.628
are also damaging their oral
health but in different ways.

00:26:17.628 --> 00:26:19.170
So during the episode
on oral health,

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:21.712
I'll spell out all the things
that you can do, most of which,

00:26:21.712 --> 00:26:24.270
by the way are completely
zero cost, many of them

00:26:24.270 --> 00:26:26.790
actually will save you money,
both in the short and long

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:29.550
term, and can really
help you improve

00:26:29.550 --> 00:26:32.490
your oral microbiome, the
strength of your teeth,

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:35.520
and reduce the number
of cavities, maybe even

00:26:35.520 --> 00:26:37.800
reverse cavities that
have begun to form

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:39.550
and are not too deep
into the tooth yet,

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:43.200
and in a really nice way, all of
that independent of your stance

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:44.160
on fluoride.

